## 5.6 Code Quality and Organization

### 5.6.1 Controller Best Practices

**Separation of Concerns**:
- Controllers handle UI logic only
- Business logic delegated to Facade
- Data persistence in DAOs

**FXML Binding**:
```java
@FXML private ListView<Book> booksListView;
@FXML private TextField searchField;
@FXML private ComboBox<String> sortComboBox;

@FXML
public void initialize() {
    setupSortStrategies();
    loadBooks();
}
```

### 5.6.2 Facade Method Usage Statistics

In the implemented controllers:

| Controller | Facade Method Calls | Most Used Methods |
|-----------|---------------------|-------------------|
| CustomerDashboardController (484 lines) | 15+ | getAllBooks(), addBookToCart(), searchBooksByTitle() |
| AdminDashboardController | 25+ | getAllOrders(), confirmOrder(), getTotalRevenue(), getOrdersByStatus() |
| CartController | 8+ | getCustomerCart(), placeOrder(), updateCartQuantity() |
| OrderHistoryController | 6+ | getCustomerOrderHistory(), cancelOrder() |
| LoginController | 3 | login(), getCurrentUser(), isAdmin() |

---

## 5.7 System Architecture Summary

The Online Book Store demonstrates a well-architected JavaFX application with:

**Layered Architecture**:
- ✓ Clear separation between View, Controller, and Model
- ✓ Facade layer simplifying subsystem access
- ✓ Service layer containing business logic
- ✓ DAO layer for data access

**Design Pattern Integration**:
- ✓ Singleton for resource management
- ✓ Factory for object creation
- ✓ Facade for simplified interface
- ✓ Strategy for flexible algorithms (HashMap-based)
- ✓ Observer for event-driven updates

**Code Organization**:
- ✓ 42 Java files logically organized
- ✓ 8 FXML views for UI
- ✓ Consistent naming conventions
- ✓ Clear package structure

**Feature Completeness**:
- ✓ All customer requirements implemented
- ✓ All admin requirements implemented
- ✓ 5 order status workflow (Pending, Confirmed, Shipped, Delivered, Canceled)
- ✓ Proper error handling
- ✓ User feedback mechanisms

